From: Tal Asa <10135422+talasa@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] fftools/ffmpeg_filter: do not elect an input the graph has already closed

What:
  choose_input() picks the filtergraph input to request the next frame from.
  It skips an input only when fgt->eof_in[i] is set, which happens once the
  EOF for that input has been delivered to the filter. close_input() (also
  run in the filter thread) sets ifp->eof when the buffersrc status says the
  graph wants no more data from that input, which can be before the EOF is
  delivered. In between, choose_input() can still elect the closed input.
  This patch skips inputs with ifp->eof set.
  It changes nothing else: upstream's pick (among the inputs still open in the
  filter thread, the one with the most failed buffersrc requests) is made
  among the inputs that are not closed, and when every input is closed it is
  made among all of them exactly as before, so the av_assert0() keeps its
  place and its conditions. The patch differs from upstream only by skipping
  closed inputs.

Why:
  Electing a closed input makes the filter thread wait on a decoder that will
  never send another frame, so ffmpeg stops making progress and never exits.
  It needs a filtergraph with many inputs where one input's last reference
  ends before the others (a 6-input graph that tiles six seeked files into one
  picture). The measurements, with their limits, are in README.md (Verification) of
  the repository that carries this patch.

Upstream:
  Not reported to FFmpeg yet.
  choose_input() is unchanged in FFmpeg master: checked at b5902e69c7 (committed 2026-10-06)
  and again at 793f6013e7 (2026-10-11).
  Drop this patch when a release carrying an equivalent fix is pinned.
---
diff --git a/fftools/ffmpeg_filter.c b/fftools/ffmpeg_filter.c
index 765b65d0ec..7b0bcf8967 100644
--- a/fftools/ffmpeg_filter.c
+++ b/fftools/ffmpeg_filter.c
@@ -2353,7 +2353,9 @@ static void send_command(FilterGraph *fg, AVFilterGraph *graph,
 static int choose_input(const FilterGraph *fg, const FilterGraphThread *fgt)
 {
     int nb_requests, nb_requests_max = -1;
+    int nb_requests_max_open = -1;
     int best_input = -1;
+    int best_input_open = -1;
 
     for (int i = 0; i < fg->nb_inputs; i++) {
         InputFilter *ifilter = fg->inputs[i];
@@ -2366,11 +2368,26 @@ static int choose_input(const FilterGraph *fg, const FilterGraphThread *fgt)
             nb_requests_max = nb_requests;
             best_input = i;
         }
+
+        // The graph has already closed this input (close_input() set
+        // ifp->eof, but fgt->eof_in is only set once the EOF reaches the
+        // filter thread). Electing it makes the filter thread wait on a
+        // decoder that will never send another frame, so prefer an input
+        // that is still open.
+        if (ifp_from_ifilter(ifilter)->eof)
+            continue;
+
+        if (nb_requests > nb_requests_max_open) {
+            nb_requests_max_open = nb_requests;
+            best_input_open = i;
+        }
     }
 
     av_assert0(best_input >= 0);
 
-    return best_input;
+    // Every input still open in the filter thread is already closed: the
+    // original pick (the most failed requests) stands.
+    return best_input_open >= 0 ? best_input_open : best_input;
 }
 
 static int choose_out_timebase(OutputFilterPriv *ofp, AVFrame *frame)
